==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
The three (in England) {{m|en|estates}} were originally the three classes of people who could participate in government, either directly or by electing representatives – originally the clergy, barons/knights, and the commons (though they changed over time). Later the "three estates" were misunderstood as being the three governmental powers necessary for legislation: the [[Crown]], the [[House of Lords]], and the [[House of Commons]]; from there, the idea of a "fourth estate" was often used in satirical or jocular expressions, before developing a fixed association with the Press.

